      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Core Entities\n- Users\n- Chats(2-100 users)\n- Messages\n- Clients (a user might have multiple devices)",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Core Entities\n- Users\n- Chats(2-100 users)\n- Messages\n- Clients (a user might have multiple devices)",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "HRKXkVIh8gDTiSf49dAiO",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -19093.451443794514,
      "y": -1392.2311842479844,
      "width": 1572.87890625,
      "height": 1550,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a3",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1682129008,
      "version": 828,
      "versionNonce": 1550830704,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783939233829,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "APIs\n- 1:1 chat is just a smaller version of group chat with 2 participants\n- REST is not a good choice here as high-frequency updates sent and received, bi-directional socket connection, Websockets!(over TLS for security)\n- a custom protocol over a raw TLS encrypted TCP connection would also work.\n- As soon as client app opens we connect it with the server which will be used to send and recieve commands which represents our API\n- So here we specify commands\n\n// -> createChat\n{\n    \"participants\": [],\n    \"name\": \"\"\n} -> {\n    \"chatId\": \"\"\n}\n\n\n// -> sendMessage\n{\n    \"chatId\": \"\",\n    \"message\": \"\",\n    \"attachments\": []\n} -> {\n    \"status\": \"SUCCESS\" | \"FAILURE\",\n    \"messageId\": \"\"\n}\n\n\n// -> createAttachment\n{\n    \"body\": ...,\n    \"hash\": \n} -> {\n    \"attachmentId\": \"\"\n}\n\n\n// -> modifyChatParticipants\n{\n    \"chatId\": \"\",\n    \"userId\": \"\",\n    \"operation\": \"ADD\" | \"REMOVE\"\n} -> \"SUCCESS\" | \"FAILURE\"\n\n\n- Each of this command will have parallel commands that are sent to other client, and other client responds with ACK letting us know they received the message.\n- This makes sure we don't loose messages\n\n\n// <- chatUpdate\n{\n    \"chatId\": \"\",\n    \"participants\": [],\n} -> \"RECEIVED\"\n\n\n// <- newMessage\n{\n    \"chatId\": \"\",\n    \"userId\": \"\"\n    \"message\": \"\",\n    \"attachments\": []\n} -> \"RECEIVED\"",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "APIs\n- 1:1 chat is just a smaller version of group chat with 2 participants\n- REST is not a good choice here as high-frequency updates sent and received, bi-directional socket connection, Websockets!(over TLS for security)\n- a custom protocol over a raw TLS encrypted TCP connection would also work.\n- As soon as client app opens we connect it with the server which will be used to send and recieve commands which represents our API\n- So here we specify commands\n\n// -> createChat\n{\n    \"participants\": [],\n    \"name\": \"\"\n} -> {\n    \"chatId\": \"\"\n}\n\n\n// -> sendMessage\n{\n    \"chatId\": \"\",\n    \"message\": \"\",\n    \"attachments\": []\n} -> {\n    \"status\": \"SUCCESS\" | \"FAILURE\",\n    \"messageId\": \"\"\n}\n\n\n// -> createAttachment\n{\n    \"body\": ...,\n    \"hash\": \n} -> {\n    \"attachmentId\": \"\"\n}\n\n\n// -> modifyChatParticipants\n{\n    \"chatId\": \"\",\n    \"userId\": \"\",\n    \"operation\": \"ADD\" | \"REMOVE\"\n} -> \"SUCCESS\" | \"FAILURE\"\n\n\n- Each of this command will have parallel commands that are sent to other client, and other client responds with ACK letting us know they received the message.\n- This makes sure we don't loose messages\n\n\n// <- chatUpdate\n{\n    \"chatId\": \"\",\n    \"participants\": [],\n} -> \"RECEIVED\"\n\n\n// <- newMessage\n{\n    \"chatId\": \"\",\n    \"userId\": \"\"\n    \"message\": \"\",\n    \"attachments\": []\n} -> \"RECEIVED\"",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "CN2SBiQQ2SiWJsNHDsGbB",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -19529.39069884996,
      "y": 284.558826735533,
      "width": 1725.9188232421875,
      "height": 500,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a4",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 363204240,
      "version": 1512,
      "versionNonce": 1362600592,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783941168040,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Notes\n- Need to use an L4 load balancer, as using WebSockets allows us to maintain persistent connection to the same server this is important to us\n- Can we use L7? Yes, now L7 also supports WebSockets but we don't need L7 capabilities, we don't need any route logic of any kind so why compromise with speed/performance.\n- Using DynamoDB as easily scalable \n- Create Chat\n    - User connects and sends a createChat message.\n    - Chat record in the database with a ChatParticipant record for each user in the chat.\n       For small chats this can be done in a single DynamoDB transaction (up to 100 items), but for chats near the 100-participant limit batch the writes.\n    - chatId returned to user\n- chat table sufficient to have chatId as PK, ChatParticipant need to (1) all participants for a given chat (2) look up all the chats for a given user.\n    - Have a composite PK with chatId is partition key and participantId is sort key\n    - Have GSI with the inverse relation i.e. participantId as partitionKey and chatId as sort key\n\nSending messges\n- When users make WebSocket connection to our server, we keep track of their connection using a simple in-memory hash map mapping userid to websocket connection\n    - User sends a sendMessage message to the Chat Server.\n    - The Chat Server looks up all participants in the chat via the ChatParticipant table.\n    - The Chat Server looks up the websocket connection for each participant in its internal hash table and sends the message via each connection.\n- Lot of assumptions here, all users are online, connected to same chat server, and we have ws connection for each of them. - To be addressed in Deep Dives\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Notes\n- Need to use an L4 load balancer, as using WebSockets allows us to maintain persistent connection to the same server this is important to us\n- Can we use L7? Yes, now L7 also supports WebSockets but we don't need L7 capabilities, we don't need any route logic of any kind so why compromise with speed/performance.\n- Using DynamoDB as easily scalable \n- Create Chat\n    - User connects and sends a createChat message.\n    - Chat record in the database with a ChatParticipant record for each user in the chat.\n       For small chats this can be done in a single DynamoDB transaction (up to 100 items), but for chats near the 100-participant limit batch the writes.\n    - chatId returned to user\n- chat table sufficient to have chatId as PK, ChatParticipant need to (1) all participants for a given chat (2) look up all the chats for a given user.\n    - Have a composite PK with chatId is partition key and participantId is sort key\n    - Have GSI with the inverse relation i.e. participantId as partitionKey and chatId as sort key\n\nSending messges\n- When users make WebSocket connection to our server, we keep track of their connection using a simple in-memory hash map mapping userid to websocket connection\n    - User sends a sendMessage message to the Chat Server.\n    - The Chat Server looks up all participants in the chat via the ChatParticipant table.\n    - The Chat Server looks up the websocket connection for each participant in its internal hash table and sends the message via each connection.\n- Lot of assumptions here, all users are online, connected to same chat server, and we have ws connection for each of them. - To be addressed in Deep Dives\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "e6Fg4hn8rt3QZoZxlBKum",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -19522.763360390483,
      "y": 815.3334821567837,
      "width": 2035.898681640625,
      "height": 475,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a5",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1126768784,
      "version": 641,
      "versionNonce": 1833226384,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783941168040,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Receive Message when not online (upto 30 days)\n- 200M DAU * 20 messages/day ~ 40K msg/sec\n- we write once to messages and once to inbox, accounting for group chats 100K writes/sec - within DynamoDB capacities\n- So steps for sender are\n    - Sender sends a sendMessage message to the Chat Server.\n    - The Chat Server looks up all participants in the chat via the ChatParticipant table.\n    - The Chat Server (a) writes the message to our Message table and (b) creates an entry in our Inbox table for each recipient.\n    - The Chat Server returns a SUCCESS or FAILURE to the sender with the final message id.\n    - The Chat Server looks up the websocket connection for each participant and attempts to deliver the message to each of them via newMessage.\n    - (For connected clients) Upon receipt, the client will send an ack message to the Chat Server to indicate they've received the message. The Chat Server will then delete the message from the Inbox table.\n- For clients that aren't connected, we keep message in inbox and as soon as client connects\n    - Look up the user's Inbox and find any undelivered message IDs.\n    - For each message ID, look up the message in the Message table.\n    - Write those messages to the client's connection via the newMessage message.\n    - Upon receipt, the client will send an ack message to the Chat Server to indicate they've received the message.\n    - The Chat Server will then delete the message from the Inbox table.\n- Finally setup a TTL or cleanup job on inbox items(30 days)\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Receive Message when not online (upto 30 days)\n- 200M DAU * 20 messages/day ~ 40K msg/sec\n- we write once to messages and once to inbox, accounting for group chats 100K writes/sec - within DynamoDB capacities\n- So steps for sender are\n    - Sender sends a sendMessage message to the Chat Server.\n    - The Chat Server looks up all participants in the chat via the ChatParticipant table.\n    - The Chat Server (a) writes the message to our Message table and (b) creates an entry in our Inbox table for each recipient.\n    - The Chat Server returns a SUCCESS or FAILURE to the sender with the final message id.\n    - The Chat Server looks up the websocket connection for each participant and attempts to deliver the message to each of them via newMessage.\n    - (For connected clients) Upon receipt, the client will send an ack message to the Chat Server to indicate they've received the message. The Chat Server will then delete the message from the Inbox table.\n- For clients that aren't connected, we keep message in inbox and as soon as client connects\n    - Look up the user's Inbox and find any undelivered message IDs.\n    - For each message ID, look up the message in the Message table.\n    - Write those messages to the client's connection via the newMessage message.\n    - Upon receipt, the client will send an ack message to the Chat Server to indicate they've received the message.\n    - The Chat Server will then delete the message from the Inbox table.\n- Finally setup a TTL or cleanup job on inbox items(30 days)\n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "KaEdI8onLoiRdYKGwXAXx",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -19521.578060109696,
      "y": 1296.6521254979298,
      "width": 1471.158935546875,
