    {
      "id": "4VNebvVOg2WkS1wV0nEIl",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -17306.927376213054,
      "y": -1677.3447996193213,
      "width": 2444.83837890625,
      "height": 1500,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a9",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 927060624,
      "version": 3635,
      "versionNonce": 1055271056,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783943595565,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How to handle billions of users simultaneously?\n\n- Single host system is convenient but unrealistic for Bil users, need to scale, challenge is how to manage websocket connections as different users on different servers\n- Need to come up with the best approach to routing requests to different servers\n\n\n1. Bad Solution : Naively horizontal scale\n- Add LB in front of all servers\nProblem : won't work, server has no knowledge of where other users are connected to and won't be able to sent it\n\n\n2. Bad Solution : Keep a kafka topic per user\n- Keep Inbox table as a Kafka topic, our chat servers will subscribe to the topic and deliver the message to the user\n- This means user connect to chat server, subscribe to the topic of their userId, when we want to send msg to a give user we publish msg to the topic for that user\n- This message would be received by all chat servers that have subscribed to that topic and then pass on msg to the websocket connection for that user\n\nProblems\n    - Does not work, Kafka is not build for billions of topic, significant overhead(50KB/topic ~ 50TB+ storage for 1B users)\n    - creating \"super topics\" that groups all users on a given chat server might help but still not a scalable good solution.\n\n\n3. Good Solution : Consistent Hashing of Chat Servers\n- Assign users to a specific Chat Server based on their userId, if done correctly we'll always know which chat server is responsible for a given user.\n- Need to keep central registry of how many Chat Servers we have, their addresses and which segments of consistent hash space they own\n- Use ZooKeeper or Etcd for this\n\nProblems\n    - Each chat Server need to maintain connection with other Chat Server to easily pass on the message meaning to make it manageable keep chat servers big in size and small in number.\n    - In case of server drop, move users without creating a thundering herd\n    - Scaling tough\n    - these problems are still solvable but you need to call them out.\n\n\n4. Great Solution : Offload to Pub/Sub\n- Have a purpose-built system to bounce msg b/w servers.\n- Redis Pub/Sub - uses a very light weight hashmap of socket connections to allow you to ferry messages to arbitrary destinations.\n- Create subscription for a give userId and publish messages to that subscription which are received \"at most Once\" by the subscribers.\n- Difference here as compared to the Kafka approach is that Redis is not managing the storage of messages\n- Kafka requires significant disk-based overhead per topic (in addition to the messages themselves) since it persists messages to disk and maintains consumer offsets.\n- Redis Pub/Sub channels are much lighter — they're essentially in-memory pointers to subscriber connections with no message persistence.\n- Need to have multiple Redis instances - we'd shard across a cluster of Redis instances (partitioning channels by user ID), with each instance handling a fraction of the total channels.\n- On connection:\n    - When users connect to our Chat Server, that server will connect to Pub/Sub to subscribe to the topic for that user ID.\n    - Any messages received on that subscription are then forwarded on to the websocket connection for that user.\n- When a message needs to be sent:\n    - We publish the message to the Pub/Sub topic for the relevant user ID.\n    - The message is received by all subscribing Chat Servers.\n    - Those Chat Servers then forward the message to the user's websocket connection.\n- Pub/Sub is \"at most once\" which means it doesn't guarantee delivery. If there's no subscribers listening or Redis has a transient failure, that message is lost.\n- This is acceptable because we write to the Inbox and Message tables before publishing to Pub/Sub. \n- The write path is:\n    - Write message to Message table + create Inbox entries (durable)\n    - Return success to sender\n    - Publish to Pub/Sub for real-time delivery (best-effort)\n- This is good approach and proved scalable by many companies\n    - Canva has a nice benchmark of this where they supported 100,000 mouse position updates per second on a single Redis host with 27% utilization!\n\nProblem : introduces extra latency as messages need to be ferried via Redis but very small cost\nNote : Kafka in the previous example was used as a persistence layer instead of messges/inbox that caused the issue, instead you can still keep the durability layer as Messages/Inbox table and use Kafka as event bus just like redis for distribution.\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How to handle billions of users simultaneously?\n\n- Single host system is convenient but unrealistic for Bil users, need to scale, challenge is how to manage websocket connections as different users on different servers\n- Need to come up with the best approach to routing requests to different servers\n\n\n1. Bad Solution : Naively horizontal scale\n- Add LB in front of all servers\nProblem : won't work, server has no knowledge of where other users are connected to and won't be able to sent it\n\n\n2. Bad Solution : Keep a kafka topic per user\n- Keep Inbox table as a Kafka topic, our chat servers will subscribe to the topic and deliver the message to the user\n- This means user connect to chat server, subscribe to the topic of their userId, when we want to send msg to a give user we publish msg to the topic for that user\n- This message would be received by all chat servers that have subscribed to that topic and then pass on msg to the websocket connection for that user\n\nProblems\n    - Does not work, Kafka is not build for billions of topic, significant overhead(50KB/topic ~ 50TB+ storage for 1B users)\n    - creating \"super topics\" that groups all users on a given chat server might help but still not a scalable good solution.\n\n\n3. Good Solution : Consistent Hashing of Chat Servers\n- Assign users to a specific Chat Server based on their userId, if done correctly we'll always know which chat server is responsible for a given user.\n- Need to keep central registry of how many Chat Servers we have, their addresses and which segments of consistent hash space they own\n- Use ZooKeeper or Etcd for this\n\nProblems\n    - Each chat Server need to maintain connection with other Chat Server to easily pass on the message meaning to make it manageable keep chat servers big in size and small in number.\n    - In case of server drop, move users without creating a thundering herd\n    - Scaling tough\n    - these problems are still solvable but you need to call them out.\n\n\n4. Great Solution : Offload to Pub/Sub\n- Have a purpose-built system to bounce msg b/w servers.\n- Redis Pub/Sub - uses a very light weight hashmap of socket connections to allow you to ferry messages to arbitrary destinations.\n- Create subscription for a give userId and publish messages to that subscription which are received \"at most Once\" by the subscribers.\n- Difference here as compared to the Kafka approach is that Redis is not managing the storage of messages\n- Kafka requires significant disk-based overhead per topic (in addition to the messages themselves) since it persists messages to disk and maintains consumer offsets.\n- Redis Pub/Sub channels are much lighter — they're essentially in-memory pointers to subscriber connections with no message persistence.\n- Need to have multiple Redis instances - we'd shard across a cluster of Redis instances (partitioning channels by user ID), with each instance handling a fraction of the total channels.\n- On connection:\n    - When users connect to our Chat Server, that server will connect to Pub/Sub to subscribe to the topic for that user ID.\n    - Any messages received on that subscription are then forwarded on to the websocket connection for that user.\n- When a message needs to be sent:\n    - We publish the message to the Pub/Sub topic for the relevant user ID.\n    - The message is received by all subscribing Chat Servers.\n    - Those Chat Servers then forward the message to the user's websocket connection.\n- Pub/Sub is \"at most once\" which means it doesn't guarantee delivery. If there's no subscribers listening or Redis has a transient failure, that message is lost.\n- This is acceptable because we write to the Inbox and Message tables before publishing to Pub/Sub. \n- The write path is:\n    - Write message to Message table + create Inbox entries (durable)\n    - Return success to sender\n    - Publish to Pub/Sub for real-time delivery (best-effort)\n- This is good approach and proved scalable by many companies\n    - Canva has a nice benchmark of this where they supported 100,000 mouse position updates per second on a single Redis host with 27% utilization!\n\nProblem : introduces extra latency as messages need to be ferried via Redis but very small cost\nNote : Kafka in the previous example was used as a persistence layer instead of messges/inbox that caused the issue, instead you can still keep the durability layer as Messages/Inbox table and use Kafka as event bus just like redis for distribution.\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "I-G0T71t3QdeP35dR7TPf",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -17303.87251368082,
      "y": -141.89595560357202,
      "width": 3184.4970703125,
      "height": 300,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aA",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 373961360,
      "version": 2208,
      "versionNonce": 1425650800,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783950399205,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Should we partition by Chat of by User?\n- Question is why to have pub/sub topics/channels per user rather than per chat?\n- For smaller groups or 1:1 chats its better to have channels per user\n    - if 1 subscription per user user1 only needs to listen to 1 topic \"user:bob\" now, if alice sends message she just need to publish to \"user:bob\", since bob is already listening to it, he reads it\n    - So 1 connection for bob\n    - Now if you make this scenario channel based subscription, server needs to listen to all the channel topics that bob is part of say 100, so when bob connected now his server needs to listen to 100 different topics for messages and you want to send message to publish to 1 channel and server takes care of delivering it.\n    - problem is with large number of 1:1 chats, if you don't go user based topics, you need to listen for all the individual chat topics that's a lot of topics to listen for a user\n- For a larger group say > 25\n    - Its smarter to have channel based listening because in this case if you go user based, you need to maintain all the listens for all the participants, where as per channel you just listen to one channel topic that multiple people are part of and don't repeat the same thing for all other participants.\n- So when users connect we list out all the chats they are part of and if the count > 25 subscribe to channels else subscribe to user\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Should we partition by Chat of by User?\n- Question is why to have pub/sub topics/channels per user rather than per chat?\n- For smaller groups or 1:1 chats its better to have channels per user\n    - if 1 subscription per user user1 only needs to listen to 1 topic \"user:bob\" now, if alice sends message she just need to publish to \"user:bob\", since bob is already listening to it, he reads it\n    - So 1 connection for bob\n    - Now if you make this scenario channel based subscription, server needs to listen to all the channel topics that bob is part of say 100, so when bob connected now his server needs to listen to 100 different topics for messages and you want to send message to publish to 1 channel and server takes care of delivering it.\n    - problem is with large number of 1:1 chats, if you don't go user based topics, you need to listen for all the individual chat topics that's a lot of topics to listen for a user\n- For a larger group say > 25\n    - Its smarter to have channel based listening because in this case if you go user based, you need to maintain all the listens for all the participants, where as per channel you just listen to one channel topic that multiple people are part of and don't repeat the same thing for all other participants.\n- So when users connect we list out all the chats they are part of and if the count > 25 subscribe to channels else subscribe to user\n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "ZtZBmm9E9VyjUdCt-P2i7",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -17296.71488845794,
      "y": 191.7044998770209,
      "width": 1224.0992431640625,
      "height": 300,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aB",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 694602352,
      "version": 948,
      "versionNonce": 399376496,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783950718894,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Handle multiple clients for a given user? (laptop + tablet + mobile)\n- Say my mobile was active and received all the messages but when later I connect my laptop I want all the messages synced.\n- Need to resolve a user to more clients that may be active at any one time\n- Need to deactivate clients so that we're not unnecessarily storing messages for a client that does not exist anymore.\n- update framework to handle multiple clients.\n- Steps:\n    - New Clients table to keep track of clients by userId\n    - When we look up participants for a chat, look up all the clients for that user\n    - update our inbox table per client rather than per-user\n    - When message sent, send to all the clients for that user\n    - On pub/sub side nothing changes\n- Add limit say 3 clients per user.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Handle multiple clients for a given user? (laptop + tablet + mobile)\n- Say my mobile was active and received all the messages but when later I connect my laptop I want all the messages synced.\n- Need to resolve a user to more clients that may be active at any one time\n- Need to deactivate clients so that we're not unnecessarily storing messages for a client that does not exist anymore.\n- update framework to handle multiple clients.\n- Steps:\n    - New Clients table to keep track of clients by userId\n    - When we look up participants for a chat, look up all the clients for that user\n    - update our inbox table per client rather than per-user\n    - When message sent, send to all the clients for that user\n    - On pub/sub side nothing changes\n- Add limit say 3 clients per user.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "QjL5AEyiinhVSlgx9VmPi",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -17288.534745346326,
      "y": 579.2387797892478,
      "width": 1637.698974609375,
      "height": 600,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aC",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1042303120,
      "version": 1879,
      "versionNonce": 760090224,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783951377581,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "What happens if the WebSocket connection fails?\n- user network poor, ws connection may technically be open but connection is functionally severed\n- We won't know about such scenario until we send a message and receive a timeout\n- TCP keepalives can take minutes to detect a dead connection, too slow\n\n1. Bad Solution : Rely on TCP Timeouts\n- do nothing special, rely on eventual timeouts and then detect, let client re-connect and sync from inbox\nProblem : TCP keepalives are slow, takes minutes, we show user connected but the connection is actually dead, missing on messages the whole time. Not good user exp.\n\n\n2. Good Solution : ACK Timeouts with Server-Side Retry\n- Chat server waits for an ACK from the client, if no ACK within short timeout(500-2000ms), the server retries delivery\n- after few failed retries, server assumes ws is broken and closes it, forcing the client to reconnect and sync\n\nProblems : Only detects failures when we're actively trying to send message, if connection dies during a quiet period we won't notice until the next message arrives.\n\n\n3. Great Solution : App level Heartbeats\n- Chat server sends periodic ping(10-30s) over ws, the client must respond with pong within a timeout(5s)\n- If no pong, server closes the connection.\n- this catches dead connection within seconds.\n\nProblem : Heartbeat is an overhead on the server with 200M active connections, with 10s heartbeat that's 20 ping/pong per sec, but not a problem.\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "What happens if the WebSocket connection fails?\n- user network poor, ws connection may technically be open but connection is functionally severed\n- We won't know about such scenario until we send a message and receive a timeout\n- TCP keepalives can take minutes to detect a dead connection, too slow\n\n1. Bad Solution : Rely on TCP Timeouts\n- do nothing special, rely on eventual timeouts and then detect, let client re-connect and sync from inbox\nProblem : TCP keepalives are slow, takes minutes, we show user connected but the connection is actually dead, missing on messages the whole time. Not good user exp.\n\n\n2. Good Solution : ACK Timeouts with Server-Side Retry\n- Chat server waits for an ACK from the client, if no ACK within short timeout(500-2000ms), the server retries delivery\n- after few failed retries, server assumes ws is broken and closes it, forcing the client to reconnect and sync\n\nProblems : Only detects failures when we're actively trying to send message, if connection dies during a quiet period we won't notice until the next message arrives.\n\n\n3. Great Solution : App level Heartbeats\n- Chat server sends periodic ping(10-30s) over ws, the client must respond with pong within a timeout(5s)\n- If no pong, server closes the connection.\n- this catches dead connection within seconds.\n\nProblem : Heartbeat is an overhead on the server with 200M active connections, with 10s heartbeat that's 20 ping/pong per sec, but not a problem.\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "Gu_sEsUSGTJNbbsYwGxVq",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -17274.21949490102,
      "y": 1268.4158369418622,
      "width": 8,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
