    },
    {
      "id": "mitt8EhYsYGLFsOpmc7P-",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -17284.56849431614,
      "y": 1284.7721289545661,
      "width": 1952.5787353515625,
      "height": 600,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aE",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 807998608,
      "version": 1722,
      "versionNonce": 334047888,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783953441187,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "What happens if Redis fails to deliver a message?\n- Redis pub/sub is atmost once, if there's no subscriber listening message won't be sent, but we've handled this by our durability layer Inbox\n- But how to ensure that connected clients quickly receive that was dropped\n\n\n1. Good Solution : Periodic Polling\n- Connected clients periodically poll server for missed messages say every 30-60s, client sends a sync request, server checks the inbox and sends the undelivered message\nProblem : with 200M connected users polling every 30s that's 7M queries/s just for sync checks\n\n\n2. Good Solution : Sequence no per chat with Gap Detection\n- each msg gets a monotonically increasing sequence number per chat and client tracks the last sequence number they've seen\n- If they receive #5 but last saw #3, they know they've missed #4 and request a re-sync\n- Generate these sequence nos in a separate Redis instance using INCR(atomic increment)\nProblem : Gap detection only works if you do receive a message, if chat goes quiet after the missed message, you won't detect the gap until the next message arrives, still need polling as a backstop.\n\n\n3. Great Solution : Piggyback Sequence on Heartbeats\n- combine heartbeats used above with sequence nos. Steps below:\n    - Global Sequence per user : Maintain a single incrementing counter per user. Every message to that user increments their sequence.\n    - Include sequence in heartbeat: When the server sends a heartbeat ping, include the user's current sequence number.\n    - Client detects gaps: If the client's local sequence is behind the server's, it immediately requests a sync.\n- Fast detection with minimal additional load as we're already sending heartbeats.\nProblem : global sequence nos per user requires atomic counter, needs co-ordination. ",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "What happens if Redis fails to deliver a message?\n- Redis pub/sub is atmost once, if there's no subscriber listening message won't be sent, but we've handled this by our durability layer Inbox\n- But how to ensure that connected clients quickly receive that was dropped\n\n\n1. Good Solution : Periodic Polling\n- Connected clients periodically poll server for missed messages say every 30-60s, client sends a sync request, server checks the inbox and sends the undelivered message\nProblem : with 200M connected users polling every 30s that's 7M queries/s just for sync checks\n\n\n2. Good Solution : Sequence no per chat with Gap Detection\n- each msg gets a monotonically increasing sequence number per chat and client tracks the last sequence number they've seen\n- If they receive #5 but last saw #3, they know they've missed #4 and request a re-sync\n- Generate these sequence nos in a separate Redis instance using INCR(atomic increment)\nProblem : Gap detection only works if you do receive a message, if chat goes quiet after the missed message, you won't detect the gap until the next message arrives, still need polling as a backstop.\n\n\n3. Great Solution : Piggyback Sequence on Heartbeats\n- combine heartbeats used above with sequence nos. Steps below:\n    - Global Sequence per user : Maintain a single incrementing counter per user. Every message to that user increments their sequence.\n    - Include sequence in heartbeat: When the server sends a heartbeat ping, include the user's current sequence number.\n    - Client detects gaps: If the client's local sequence is behind the server's, it immediately requests a sync.\n- Fast detection with minimal additional load as we're already sending heartbeats.\nProblem : global sequence nos per user requires atomic counter, needs co-ordination. ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "8jZFgpMiRW9g-sAcxu48r",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -17288.534745346296,
      "y": 1979.0657697624351,
      "width": 1796.7388916015625,
      "height": 125,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aF",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1330934928,
      "version": 590,
      "versionNonce": 1473874576,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783953706015,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How to handle out-of-order messages?\n- out-of-order messages can't be ignored in distributed systems, need to have delays to ensure we have time for late messages to arrive and a re-ordering mech to order'em\n- All chat server sync their time over NTP(Network Time Protocol), doesn't guarantee perfect time but good\n- When msg received on server we stamp it with time it was received, when client get that message they have at what time server received it, while displaying we order them by this TS\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How to handle out-of-order messages?\n- out-of-order messages can't be ignored in distributed systems, need to have delays to ensure we have time for late messages to arrive and a re-ordering mech to order'em\n- All chat server sync their time over NTP(Network Time Protocol), doesn't guarantee perfect time but good\n- When msg received on server we stamp it with time it was received, when client get that message they have at what time server received it, while displaying we order them by this TS\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "aK4qWpqz0JklQZNRaCnfK",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -17279.33208434575,
      "y": 2188.6819369972786,
      "width": 2828.878173828125,
      "height": 500,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aG",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1710259312,
      "version": 1172,
      "versionNonce": 655049328,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783954183007,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How to handle \"last-seen\" functionality?\n\n1. Bad Solution : Write to DB on every heartbeat\n- update lastSeen TS in DB everytime a user does anything: sends message, receives msg basically responds to a heartbeat\nProblems : Too much data to store even DynamoDB would struggle\nthis data is also stale immediately after writing\n\n\n2. Great Solution : Utilise Active Connections\n- We know when users connect to our Chat Server and when they disconnect through Heartbeats\n- Keep a table in DynamoDB that just stores their last disconnect time for a given user\n- Whenever a user disconnects from a Chat Server, we update this value with current TS, and use conditional update - only update if current TS is > existing one.\n- Steps Below : \n    - The client publishes a getLastSeen message with their targetUserId and the requestingUserId.\n    - The Chat Server receives the message. In parallel, it will: 2a) Check the LastSeen table for the targetUserId and publish a updateLastSeen message to the Pub/Sub channel for the requestingUserId. 2b) Forward a getLastSeen message to the Pub/Sub channel for the targetUserId.\n    - If the target user's Chat Server receives the getLastSeen and the user is connected, it will publish an updateLastSeen message with lastSeen of \"ONLINE\" to the Pub/Sub channel for the requestingUserId.\n    - Finally, the client will merge the responses. If it receives an ONLINE message, the bubble is green. If it doesn't, it will show when the user last disconnected from the service.\n- This approach minimises the storage and no of update rquired.\nProblem : there could be some delay b/w two responses for an online user, client needs to wait for it before displaying the result.\n    ",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How to handle \"last-seen\" functionality?\n\n1. Bad Solution : Write to DB on every heartbeat\n- update lastSeen TS in DB everytime a user does anything: sends message, receives msg basically responds to a heartbeat\nProblems : Too much data to store even DynamoDB would struggle\nthis data is also stale immediately after writing\n\n\n2. Great Solution : Utilise Active Connections\n- We know when users connect to our Chat Server and when they disconnect through Heartbeats\n- Keep a table in DynamoDB that just stores their last disconnect time for a given user\n- Whenever a user disconnects from a Chat Server, we update this value with current TS, and use conditional update - only update if current TS is > existing one.\n- Steps Below : \n    - The client publishes a getLastSeen message with their targetUserId and the requestingUserId.\n    - The Chat Server receives the message. In parallel, it will: 2a) Check the LastSeen table for the targetUserId and publish a updateLastSeen message to the Pub/Sub channel for the requestingUserId. 2b) Forward a getLastSeen message to the Pub/Sub channel for the targetUserId.\n    - If the target user's Chat Server receives the getLastSeen and the user is connected, it will publish an updateLastSeen message with lastSeen of \"ONLINE\" to the Pub/Sub channel for the requestingUserId.\n    - Finally, the client will merge the responses. If it receives an ONLINE message, the bubble is green. If it doesn't, it will show when the user last disconnected from the service.\n- This approach minimises the storage and no of update rquired.\nProblem : there could be some delay b/w two responses for an online user, client needs to wait for it before displaying the result.\n    ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
